 ## Request lifecycle
 
-HTTP requests enter through the edge proxy, are authenticated by the session
-service, and are handed to the API server. The API reads and writes Postgres
-directly. There is no cache: every read goes to the database.
+HTTP requests enter through the edge proxy and are authenticated by the session
+service. The API server reads through a Redis cache in front of Postgres; a miss
+loads from the database and fills the cache for five minutes.
 
 ## Background work
 
-A scheduled worker polls the outbox table every minute and delivers queued email.
-Failures are retried three times with a fixed delay.
+The API writes email jobs to Postgres. A queue consumer picks them up with
+`SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`, delivers them, and marks the row done.
+Redelivery after a crash is expected, so handlers must be idempotent.
 
 ## Deployments
 
-The API and the worker deploy from the same image. A deploy runs migrations
-first, then replaces instances one at a time behind the proxy.
+The API and the consumer deploy from the same image. A deploy runs migrations
+first, then replaces instances one at a time behind the proxy. The cache is not
+flushed: entries expire on their own.
